  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>A molecule&nbsp;is said to be electron deficient because there are not enough valence electrons to form the expected number of covalent bonds. For example, in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></math>, there are only&nbsp;12&nbsp;electrons, three from each boron atoms and six from hydrogen, while ethane possesses&nbsp;14&nbsp;such electrons.</p><p>Also in&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>,&nbsp;B&nbsp;has six electrons in its outermost orbit. So, the number of electron deficient molecules are 2.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
